  "body": "### Summary\nAs a visitor, contributor or donor, I want the README of `dev-swift` to show where SwiftHFR stands today, what is planned, how it is built and how to take part, and to ask clearly for support. Operator request, 2026-11-08.\n\n### Scope (README.md on dev-swift, plus what it links to)\n1. **Support note at the top.** Move the Leetchi badge up, under the title, inside a GitHub `> [!NOTE]` block that asks plainly for support.\n   - Name the costs: the Apple Developer account ($99 per year) and the image-hosting API ($170 per year).\n   - Say that maintaining the app is a shared effort: AI subscriptions, software tools, hardware and time.\n   - The `## Support` section at the bottom stays as a short pointer, or merges into the note.\n2. **\"Where we are\" from the roadmap issues.**\n   - The milestone table must match the boards of #166 (M1) and #324 (M2–M5).\n   - If that table, or the SVG, is generated by `tooling/roadmap/` (`render.py`, `generate.py`), fix the generator and regenerate. Never hand-edit generated output.\n   - M5 must be shown like M1–M4: same row format, same progress figure, and its explanation paragraph aligned with the others.\n3. **M5 pass.**\n   - Read the M5 lots (`docs/LOTS.md`, the M5 rows; `tooling/roadmap/m5.py`) and their issues, including ezzz's recent comments on them.\n   - Add a short \"Big topics planned\" list (about 5 to 8 bullets, grouped by theme) that links to the issues.\n   - Report on the issues any lot status that ezzz's comments make stale. The README is not the tracker.\n4. **\"How it is built\" refresh.** Bring it in line with current practice:\n   - orchestrator, sub-orchestrators and child sessions;\n   - claims;\n   - adversarial reviews by another model, with the model routing;\n   - CodeRabbit;\n   - macOS runners as the bottleneck, drafts first (#477);\n   - Mac sessions for captures and device checks;\n   - the rule #620 ledger: GAP and CHANGELOG entries in the PR body, written by a steward;\n   - merge policy.\n\n   Link to AGENTS.md, ORCHESTRATION, REVIEW-ROUNDS and CONTRIBUTING instead of copying them.\n5. **Participation guidelines, human and agentic.** From the README, directly or by one link each, a newcomer must find every step:\n   - pick an issue (`status: ready`, `external: ready`);\n   - claim it with `/claim <session-id>`, using the session-id format;\n   - get the `status: claimed` label;\n   - write the PR body: `Closes` or `Refs #N`, `Claim:`, `LOT-XXX`, `Direct-PR:`;\n   - get the review (`REVIEW WANTED: <model>`), handle CodeRabbit, meet the merge criteria, and know who merges;\n   - write the ledger;\n   - release with `/release`;\n   - respect the per-contributor PR limits;\n   - follow the EXTERNAL-AGENTS brief.\n\n   Fill the gaps in CONTRIBUTING / EXTERNAL-AGENTS only where a step is missing. Keep one source per rule (AGENTS.md \"Documentation rules\").\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n- Every number and status in the README comes from the tracker or the generator. Cite the source in the PR.\n- No copied rules: links instead.\n- Docs-only PR (no `App/` or `Packages/` changes, so no macOS jobs).\n- Opus review.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
